<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/10/wikilinking-header-rb.7537.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p>Wiki links now work on <a href="https://share.markedapp.com">Marked Share</a>. Type a note&rsquo;s name in double brackets and, when someone reads the page, that name becomes a link to the matching document. The match stays inside the same library folder, so two notes called &ldquo;Meeting&rdquo; in different folders do not steal each other&rsquo;s links.</p>

<h3 id="links-that-stay-in-the-folder">Links that stay in the folder</h3>

<p>The brackets stay in the Markdown you save. The share page is what rewrites them, which means a renamed note or a newly shared sibling shows up the next time the page is rendered. No republish required.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>See [[Meeting notes]] for the recap.
The short version is in [[Meeting notes|the notes]].
Jump to [[Meeting notes#Decisions]].</code></pre></div></div>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[[Meeting notes]]</code> links to the document whose name in that folder is &ldquo;Meeting notes&rdquo;. An alias after <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">|</code> is only the visible text. A <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#heading</code> jumps to that heading on the target page.</p>

<p>Matching is case-insensitive when there is only one candidate. If two notes differ only by case, the exact spelling wins. A private note is linked for its owner. Everyone else still sees the brackets.</p>

<h3 id="a-missing-note-if-it-is-yours">A missing note, if it is yours</h3>

<p>When you&rsquo;re signed in and looking at your own document, a wiki link with no match turns red. Click it and Marked Share creates a private note in the same folder and opens the editor with that title already filled in.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>[[Follow up]]</code></pre></div></div>

<p>If &ldquo;Follow up&rdquo; does not exist yet, that red link becomes a new note titled &ldquo;Follow up&rdquo;. Other readers still see <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[[Follow up]]</code> as plain text. Once the note exists, the link is a normal share link &mdash; a private note still only links for the logged in owner, a public note becomes a public cross-link.</p>

<h3 id="a-list-when-you-type-">A list when you type <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[[</code></h3>

<p>The editor knows the other documents in the current folder. Type <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[[</code> and a list pops up. Keep typing and the list narrows.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>See [[Meet</code></pre></div></div>

<p>That offers &ldquo;Meeting notes&rdquo; and anything else in the folder whose name contains &ldquo;meet&rdquo;. The list follows the folder selected on the document, so switching folders changes the suggestions.</p>

<h3 id="backlinks">Backlinks</h3>

<p>Since I was working on wiki linking, I also added a backlink accumulator for wiki-linked documents. If a document is linked to by other documents, it gets a &ldquo;Backlinks&rdquo; tab on the right, which expands to a panel of linking documents when clicked.</p>

<h3 id="reference-ids-both-directions">Reference ids, both directions</h3>

<p>Reference-style links got the same kind of help. If the document already has definitions, the editor can fill in the id while you are still inside the link.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code>See <span class="p">[</span><span class="nv">the spec</span><span class="p">][</span><span class="ss">spec</span><span class="p">]</span> and <span class="p">[</span><span class="nv">CommonMark</span><span class="p">][]</span>.

<span class="p">[</span><span class="ss">spec</span><span class="p">]:</span> <span class="sx">https://example.com/spec</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>With the cursor in <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[the spec][|]</code>, the list is every <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[id]: url</code> line in the document. The same list opens for an image, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">![diagram][|]</code>. Typing filters it, and choosing an id drops it in with the closing bracket.</p>

<p>The other direction is for the definition you have not written yet. Put a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[</code> within three spaces of the start of a line and the list is every id used in the text that still needs a URL. That includes <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[label][id]</code>, image references, and collapsed links like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[CommonMark][]</code>, whose id is the link text. Ids that already have a definition stay out of that list, and fenced code blocks are ignored.</p>

<p>So I can drop <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[some claim][src]</code> in the paragraph, scroll down, type <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">[</code>, and pick <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">src</code> when I am ready to paste the URL.</p>

<h3 id="roadmap">Roadmap</h3>

<p>These are just some quality of life improvements for Marked Share. I&rsquo;m still waiting to see if it takes off and I have to start charging for premium features. My thought is that I&rsquo;d charge other app developers a white label fee to be able to sync and publish, rather than requiring the end user to pay a separate subscription fee. I built this to add publishing to <a href="https://markedapp.com">Marked 3</a>, and wanted to offer that for free to existing users. And I wanted anyone to be able to read and review without needing an account. So I think a white label fee would be the best way to make it sustainable.</p>

<p>Honestly, it&rsquo;s not costing me much to run right now, and it&rsquo;s adding a valuable feature to my paid apps, so I&rsquo;m not too worried about it at this point. The only thing that would really hurt my pocketbook is if people started uploading a lot of additional assets that I needed to host. We&rsquo;ll see if it gets to that point.</p>

<p>I&rsquo;m considering Share to be feature complete at this point. That&rsquo;s not to say there won&rsquo;t be constant improvements and new features, I just think it&rsquo;s at a great 1.0 place. It&rsquo;s stable and reliable. If you develop a Markdown app that could use publishing (or entire folder sync), please reach out. If you&rsquo;re an end user who thinks Marked Share would fit well in an app you use, let me know!</p>

